==Latgalian==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|ltg|ine-bsl-pro|*bolʔto}}/{{m|ine-bsl-pro|*baltan}} from {{etyl|ine-pro|ltg}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰel-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*bʰol-||shiny, white}}. Cognates include {{cog|lv|balts}}, {{cog|lt|báltas}}, {{etyl|xsv|-}} {{m|sv|baltas}}. In several Indo-European languages, reflexes of the stem {{m|ine-pro|*bʰel-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*bʰol-}} are often found in words relating to water or humid places, probably due to their shiny, reflective surfaces: {{cog|xil|*balta}} (“marsh, swamp”), {{cog|sq|baltë||mud, sludge, swamp}}, Proto-Slavic {{m|sla-pro|*bolto||swamp, lake}} ({{cog|cu|блато||lake}}, {{cog|ru|болото||marsh, swamp}} (dialectal “puddle, lake”), {{cog|cs|bláto||mud; pl. swamp}}, {{cog|pl|błoto||mud, swamp}}). This usage is also attested in Baltic languages, as in, e.g., {{etyl|prg|-}} placename {{m|prg|Namuynbalt}} (swamp).

